{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Primitive Data Types in Java "}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use when you are discussing about the primitive data types in Java. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand important information about client software for the identity metasystem in spoken or written documents."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Perfect versus Past Simple: Questions and Affirmative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Present Perfect as well as the <storng>Past Simple</strong> on a daily basis. They are important to express yourself in a natural way about events that took place in the past."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Present Perfect</strong><br/>Using the Present Perfect implies the idea that the event you are refering to started at an unknown moment in the past and that it continued until the present. Plus, you do not know when this event or action will finish.<br/><br/>Remember that there is an auxiliary verb,<b> have/has</b>, which is needed in this tense and that changes according to the pronoun being used. Plus, the verbs have to be conjugated in the past participle form.<br/><br/>In this table you can see the way you use the auxiliary verb have:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":7,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I\\t\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Has\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Has\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"It\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Has\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Have\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "For affirmative sentences, you have to follow the structure:<br/><br/><div class=\"center_text\">Subject + has/have + verb  (past participle form) + complement</div><br/><br/>Example:<br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">I <strong>have used</strong> Java.</span><br/><br/>For questions, the structure changes depending on the type of question.<br/><br/>For yes/no questions follow the structure:<br/><br/><div class=\"center_text\">Have /Has + subject + verb (past participle) + complement + ?</div>Example:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><strong>Have</strong> you <strong>used</strong> primitive data types?</span><br/><br/>For open questions, follow the structure:<br/><br/><div class=\"center_text\">Wh- + have /has + subject + verb (past participle) + complement + ?</div><br/><br/>Example:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><strong>What</strong> primitive data types <strong>have</strong> you <strong>used</strong> before?</span><br/><br/><strong>SIMPLE PAST<br/></strong>Using the Simple Past tense implies the idea that the event or action that you are making reference to started and finished at a specific moment in the past. It is no longer happening in the present.<br/><br/>Remember that depending on the type of verb, regular or irregular, it will change in its Simple Past form. Plus, the interrogative form must include the auxiliary verb \u201cdid\u201d.<br/><br/>For affirmative sentences use the following structure:<br/><br/><div class=\"center_text\">Subject + verb in past +  complement</div><br/><br/>Example:<br/><span class=\"italic\">I <strong>used</strong> the primitive data types last class.</span><br/><br/>For questions, the structure changes depending on the type of question. For yes/no questions follow the structure:<br/><br/><div class=\"center_text\">Did + subject + verb (base form) + complement + ?</div><br/><br/>Example:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><strong>Did</strong> you <strong>follow</strong> the instructions for Java?</span><br/><br/>For open questions, follow the structure:<br/><br/><div class=\"center_text\">Wh- + did + subject + verb (base form) + complement + ?</div><br/><br/>Example:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><strong>Why did</strong> you <strong>program</strong> this using Java?</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Larry, what have you learnt in the past weeks? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "What did you do to learn so much about that?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Did you get the idea?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "What have you learned these days?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Have Got to for What Is Necessary (Negative Form)"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Have got to</strong>, in its negative form, shows the idea that something is not necessary."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you use <strong>have got to</strong> in the negative form, the idea that something is not necessary comes with it. You are mentioning that there is no need to proceed in a specific way regarding a situation.  </br></br>  The structure that you follow to create negative sentences with this expression is: </br></br>  Subject + have/has + not + got to + verb (base form) + complement. </br></br>  Example: </br></br>  <span class=\"italic\">These systems <strong>haven't got to consume</strong> so many bytes. </br> He <strong>hasn't got to study</strong> the Boolean Data Type. </br></span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You haven't got to memorize them."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Now, if you want to become knowledgeable about this area, you haven't got to forget that we have different primitive data types."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "underestimate "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to not understand the real importance or value of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, you are not going to have to keep underestimating those because they are extremely important in the systems field."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "field "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a specific area of study "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, you are not going to have to keep underestimating it because they are extremely important in the systems field."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "knowledgeable "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the characteristic of knowing a lot of information about something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Now, if you want to become knowledgeable about this area, you haven't got to forget that we have different primitive data types."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "numeric data type "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "one of the two categories of the primitive data type which presents subcategories that include different numeric values and corresponding memory bytes"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I remember that we had two main categories, the Boolean and Numeric data types, from which we got subcategories such as the character, the integral, the floating-point-group and the integer group, among others."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "outcome "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the result of a process or action"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sure, each data type provides the client with a service and an outcome."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "desire "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to want to achieve or reach something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I recall that you used the Boolean Data Type which allowed you to work with two different possible values, true or false, in order to assess the conditions that allowed you to come up with the desired output for the client."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob01aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more details about primitive data types."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a mind map with all the information you find."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your mind map with the class."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I search information about primitive data types."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a mind map showing details about primitive data types."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I discuss my map with my partners"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob01re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Piper: </strong> Larry, what have you learned these days? </br>\n<strong>Larry: </strong> In school, we have checked the primitive data types in Java. </br>\n<strong>Piper: </strong> I didn't learn much about systems and all that stuff. </br>\n<strong>Larry: </strong> Well, you are not going to have to keep underestimating those because they are extremely important in the systems field. These primitive data types are the most basic ones in Java and allow people to contain pure and simple values; furthermore, each data type serves a unique purpose that cannot be changed and that adjusts, throughout codes, to the particular system's needs. </br></br>"}, {"time": "37000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob01re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Piper: </strong> What did you do to learn so much about that? </br>\n<strong>Larry: </strong> I just studied. Now, if you want to become knowledgeable about this area, you haven't got to forget that we have different primitive data types. I remember that we had two main categories, the Boolean and Numeric data types, from which we got subcategories such as the character, the integral, the floating-point-group and the integer group, among others. You haven't got to memorize them. Did you get the idea? </br></br>"}, {"time": "69000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob01re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Piper: </strong> You are not going to make me understand that unless you give me some examples. </br>\n<strong>Larry: </strong> Sure, each data type provides the client with a service and an outcome. I recall that you used the Boolean Data Type which allowed you to work with two different possible values, true or false, in order to assess the conditions that allowed you to come up with the desired output for the client. On the other hand, within the numeric data types, we had the Character type which is the only one that allows people to include any type of character or symbol within the code. All the rest represents actual numeric values in bytes of memory consumption."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le02ob01re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le02ob01re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the importance of primitive data types in Java."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list different types of primitive data in Java. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can give characteristics of different types of primitive data in Java to offer a service to a client."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What is Java useful for? Share your thoughts. "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What is the relationship between codes and Java? Explain. "}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "Before writing codes, there's something you have to know\u2026"}], "img_path": "", "id": 0}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "temp